    {
      "title": "Comparisons and alternatives",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "The mongodump archive format interleaves collection data, allowing concurrent collection backup and restore through a single file or stream; it is a special-purpose format rather than a tar archive. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/company/blog/technical/archiving-and-compression-in-mongodb-tools",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:cdc861f7e3caa71b0b2cc3c02e64bd943bf85b8367cd9c861d34f6a611a7710e",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:a3478d0de4ec30f46bfdef604c8290e517276f20884c6d3874660c1b53285b88",
            "ev:e717112166bfbf05810d1c8f4b2a9e6ad0e2465900d36d86f8d8c95b6eade367",
            "ev:ee74b538273f816b2d878972cb2480fe47f572a70e5c8c9e789c4e4df5538f7f"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://www.mongodb.com/company/blog/engineering/multiplexing-golang-channels-maximize-throughput"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "For a single-collection restore, mongorestore -j maps to --numInsertionWorkersPerCollection rather than --numParallelCollections. Explicit long flags make the intended concurrency control clear. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/database-tools/mongorestore.md",
          "note": null,
